# The annotations of the game image $5B00-$FE00 (code2 $5B00-$6AFF, tape block 7 moved by the mover; code1
# $6B00-$FE00, tape block 6): the single source of truth for src/game/*.asm (kit/mkgame.py --case .). Format:
# ../../../../docs/ann-format.md.
#
# Witness: "L1 N" is the number of runs of an instruction summed over c1 (power-on with the tape) and the ten bot
# recordings of work/stage1/rec (tools, ports, menus, growth, city, city2, metro, disasters, levels, bankrupt), from
# work/stage2/counts.json (work/stage2/l1count.py); data reads and writes are the ten bot builds' sums
# (work/stage2/rw.py). "L1 -" or "known only from the code" marks what no build ran.
# Coordinates: the map is 96 x 96 cells, cell (column x, row y) at CityMap + 96 y + x; the view shows 32 x 16 cells
# from column ViewCol, row ViewRow on the screen's rows 8-23. The pointer moves in half-character steps across
# (PointerX 0-62) and in pixel pairs down (PointerY 0-184).
#
# The two tasks: the UI task runs UiTask / UiLoop ($8FA0, $8FCC; the pass UiPass $8FDA) on the stack from $F6C2
# (UiStack $F682-$F6C1), the simulation task SimTask ($835F) on its own (from $F704 down over SimFrame and SimStack, reached down to $F6D0 in the recordings); the interrupt
# (Interrupt $F70B) swaps them every frame unless SwitchOff $F705 is set.

@equ $4000 Screen 6144 ; the display file
@equ $5800 Attributes 768 ; the attributes
@equ $0562 RomLdBytesIn ; the ROM's LD-BYTES after its border set-up (LD-BYTES + 12), used by the save and load
@equ $04C6 RomSaBytesOut ; the ROM's SA-BYTES after its header choice (the pilot, $04C6), used by the save
@const RomFont $3C00 ; the ROM's character set less 256 (character n at RomFont + 8 n)

@isub $F75A LD BC,RomFont
@isub $5B1C LD SP,UiStack+$40
@isub $5B2B LD HL,$8000
@isub $5E41 LD DE,-10000
@isub $5E47 LD DE,-1000
@isub $65D2 LD DE,$BDC0
@isub $65DC LD DE,$7960
@isub $65E6 LD DE,-10000
@isub $65F0 LD DE,-1000
@isub $A801 LD DE,-10000
@isub $A807 LD DE,-1000
@isub $72F2 LD DE,40000

@inline $5DEF
@inline $6704


@module entry ; The keyboard rows and the entry
@about The first bytes of code2: the eight keyboard half rows the interrupt reads every frame (ReadInput $F781) and the joysticks fold into, then the entry the mover jumps to: the stack, the Kempston test, IM 2 with I = $FD (the vector table $FD00, the task switcher Interrupt $F70B), the start menu.
$5B00 b KeyRows 8 ; The keyboard's eight half rows, as IN read them in the last interrupt (0 = pressed, bits 0-4)
> ReadInput $F781 writes them every frame from ports $FEFE, $FDFE, $FBFE, $F7FE, $EFFE, $DFFE, $BFFE, $7FFE (in that order), then ReadKempston $7A01 and ReadSinclair $7A32 clear the Q, A, O, P and SPACE bits when a joystick is pushed, so every reader sees one keyboard. Row 0 bit 0 CAPS SHIFT, row 1 bit 0 A (down), row 2 bit 0 Q (up), row 5 bit 0 P (right) and bit 1 O (left), row 7 bit 0 SPACE (fire) and bit 1 SYMBOL SHIFT. L1: 3 264 254 writes (8 a frame), 5 764 078 reads with KeyNone and BitMasks.
$5B01 KeyRowA ; $FDFE: A S D F G
$5B02 KeyRowQ ; $FBFE: Q W E R T
$5B03 KeyRow1 ; $F7FE: 1 2 3 4 5
$5B04 KeyRow0 ; $EFFE: 0 9 8 7 6
$5B05 KeyRowP ; $DFFE: P O I U Y
$5B06 KeyRowEnter ; $BFFE: ENTER L K J H
$5B07 KeyRowSpace ; $7FFE: SPACE SYMBOL SHIFT M N B
$5B08 KeyNone ; a ninth row, always $FF: ScanKey reads nine rows
$5B09 PortHighs ; leftover: the eight port high bytes $FE-$7F; no code reads them (L1: 0 reads)
$5B11 BitMasks ; $80, $40, ... $01: the bit of a column, read by PowerBit $75F1 (the power map's bit of a cell) (L1: 1 415 565 reads)
$5B19 EntryJump ; JR $5B1B, two bytes no code of the tape version runs (L1: never executed): the tape's mover reaches the entry by JP $5B1B; the +3 disk's boot enters here (JP $5B19, work/stage2/NOTES.md 5.3)

$5B1B c Entry ; The game's entry: stack, black border, the Kempston test, IM 2 with I = $FD, then UiTask
> MoveCode2 $5000 jumps here. SP = $F6C2 (the top of the UI task's stack UiStack), the border black, SwitchOff $F705 = 1 (no task switch until the simulation task is set up), then the Kempston test: port $1F is read up to 32 768 times; a value $1F or more (a floating bus gives $FF) means no interface, otherwise KempstonFound = 1. Then I = $FD (the vector table VectorTable $FD00, every vector $FCFC), IM 2, EI and JP UiTask $8FA0.
> c1: L1 1 at frame 16 280; the loop ran 32 768 times (every read below $1F: c1's setup has a Kempston interface, KempstonFound = 1). No other code changes I or the mode.
$5B2E KempstonTest ; IN A,($1F): below $1F an interface answers
$5B3E SetIm2 ; I = $FD, IM 2, EI, JP UiTask

@module beeper ; The two beeper routines: the disaster noise and the refusal tone
@about NoiseBurst (the disasters, through FireNoise, FloodNoise, CrashNoise and the earthquake) and ToneBeep (the two refusals of the tools, Refuse $71E4). Both write the speaker bit of port $FE directly. NoiseBurst runs with interrupts off (DI ... EI), so the frame's interrupt waits; ToneBeep leaves them on.
$5B48 c NoiseBurst ; The disaster noise: 200 loops of the beeper from Random, gated by the SOUND option
> Input: E the pitch mask (each loop sends a new random speaker bit when D AND E is 0: E = 0 every loop, 1 every second ...). Interrupts are off while it plays (DI ... EI), so the frame's interrupt is held and the task switch waits. Returns at once when SoundOn ($A32D) is 0, but after the DI: with SOUND off it returns with interrupts off, and they stay off until the next EI (a bug of the original). Callers: the disasters (Disaster $78BD, the fire and flood spread $9108, $9116).
> L1 764 (the disasters recording: 127-255 calls a disaster), 30 406 random bits sent.
$5B53 NoiseLoop ; INC D; D AND E = 0: a new random bit to the speaker
$5B5F NoiseNext ; 200 iterations

$5B66 c ToneBeep ; The refusal tone: 500 cycles of the speaker, 48 + 16 DJNZ loops a cycle (the beep of a refused tool)
> No inputs. The speaker on with L = $30 DJNZ loops, off with H = $10, 500 times (DE = $01F4): 500 cycles of 889 T, 444 500 T uncontended, 127 ms; measured 164 ms (8.2 frames, tools frame 13 434), with contention and the interrupts. It is not gated by SoundOn. Reached by JP ToneBeep from Refuse $71E4 when a placement is refused (a tool too dear is refused silently by SelectTool's RET M). L1 3: 2 in tools (the occupied cell at frame 13 434 traced, the other not located), 1 in bankrupt.
$5B6C ToneCycle ; speaker on for L loops
$5B71 ToneHigh ; DJNZ: the high half
$5B77 ToneLow ; DJNZ: the low half

@module input ; The Kempston flag and the key scans
@about ReadPort1F, the key characters and ScanKey, KeyToChar and AnyKey: the typed input (the name and gene editor), the map keys and the "any key" waits.
$5B7F b KempstonFound 1 ; 1 when Entry found a Kempston interface (port $1F reads below $1F), else 0
> Read by ReadPort1F $5B80 every frame (L1 413 183 reads).

$5B80 c ReadPort1F ; A = the Kempston port's bits inverted, or $FF when no interface
> Output: A = NOT IN ($1F) when KempstonFound, else $FF (CPL of 0). Called by ReadKempston $7A01 from the interrupt (L1 413 183, one a frame).
$5B88 ReadPort1FDone ; CPL

$5B8A b KeyChars 41 ; The character of each key, five a half row in ScanKey's order (CAPS SHIFT first), then the key found
> Row $FEFE from bit 0: $03 (CAPS SHIFT), Z X C V; $FDFE A S D F G; $FBFE Q W E R T; $F7FE 1-5; $EFFE 0 9 8 7 6; $DFFE P O I U Y; $BFFE $08 (ENTER as delete), L K J H; $7FFE SPACE, ".", M N B (SYMBOL SHIFT reads as "."). KeyFound at $5BB2 is ScanKey's result: the key's index, or $FF for none. Read by KeyToChar $5BDF (L1 1193 reads).
$5BB2 KeyFound ; ScanKey's result: the index into KeyChars of the first key down, $FF for none

$5BB3 c ScanKey ; Find the first key down in the nine rows: KeyFound = its index (carry clear), or $FF (carry set)
> Reads KeyRows $5B00-$5B08; CAPS SHIFT is ignored in the first row (D = $FE masks it). Output: carry set and KeyFound = $FF when no key is down, else carry clear and KeyFound = 5 row + column. L1 5451.
$5BBB ScanRow ; the row's five bits, without CAPS SHIFT in the first
$5BD2 ScanBit ; find the bit
$5BDB ScanNextBit ; one column on

$5BDE b ScanSpare 1 ; One byte no code reads (L1: 0 reads); kept as bytes

$5BDF c KeyToChar ; A = the character of the key down (KeyChars), carry set when none
> Used by the typed input: the city name (InputName $A6F9), the gene digits and the tool shortcut keys (ToolKeys $6D2D). L1 5451, 1193 with a key.

$5BEF c AnyKey ; Z clear when any key of the nine rows is down
> Output: A = the first row's bits inverted (NZ) or 0 with Z. L1 2523 (the menus' key repeat, NumberRepeat $6A31).
$5BF4 AnyKeyRow ; the next row

@module screen ; Screen addresses, icon drawing and clearing
@about ScreenAddr and AttrAddr, the next pixel line, DrawIcon from IconTiles and IconAttrs, and the clears of the top third, the view area and the maps window.
$5BFE c ScreenAddr ; HL = the display file address of character row B (0-23), column C (0-31)
> No other register changes but A. L1 451 620: every print and drawing routine calls it.
$5BFF ScreenAddrThird ; the third: B AND $18 OR $40
$5C01 ScreenAddrHigh
$5C03 ScreenAddrH
$5C04 ScreenAddrRow ; the row in the third: (B AND 7) x 32
$5C05 ScreenAddrRowBits
$5C07 ScreenAddrRot1
$5C08 ScreenAddrRot2
$5C09 ScreenAddrRot3
$5C0A ScreenAddrCol ; OR C
$5C0B ScreenAddrL

$5C0D c AttrAddr ; HL = the attribute address of row B, column C (C kept, B = $58)
> L1 1024 (the menu highlight HighlightLine $6933 and the maps window's HighlightMapKey / HighlightLayer).

$5C19 c NextPixelLine ; HL = the display file address one pixel line down
> The usual three cases: inside the character (INC H), into the next character row (L + 32, H - 8), into the next third (L + 32 with carry). L1 3 121 944.

$5C28 c DrawIcon ; Draw icon tile A (8 bytes at IconTiles + 8 A) at row B, column C, with its attribute from IconAttrs
> Sets PrintAddr to the cell, copies the eight bytes down the cell and the attribute IconAttrs + A ($F4F8 + A) through PutAttr $F774. Used for the tool palette, the box frames and the window graphics. L1 36 344.
$5C43 DrawIconLoop

$5C57 c DrawIconHere ; Draw icon tile A at PrintAddr with the current PrintAttr (no position, no own attribute)
> Then JP PrintGlyphAttr $F76E (PrintAttr $FF leaves the attribute). L1 27 668 (the box frames of DrawBoxRow).
$5C67 DrawIconHereLoop

$5C70 c ClearTopBlack ; Clear the top third ($4000-$47FF) and give it attribute $38
> A = $38 (white paper, black ink) into $5800-$58FF. L1 40.
$5C74 c ClearTopWhite ; Clear the top third with attribute $07 (black paper, white ink)
> L1 28.
$5C76 ClearTopA ; the pixels zeroed, then the attributes A

$5C8F c ClearViewArea ; Clear the screen's rows 8-23 ($4800-$57FF), attribute $47 (bright white on black)
> The map view's area, cleared before a window or a dialog is drawn over it. L1 223.

$5CAA c ClearForMaps ; Clear the top third black on white, then every attribute row: 8 columns $07, 24 columns $21
> Called by the maps window ($7A9E) before it draws a map: the left 8 columns keep a black key, the 24 on the right are blue on red ($21). L1 5.
$5CB2 MapsAttrRow
$5CB4 MapsAttrKey
$5CBB MapsAttrMap

@module random ; The random generator
@about Random: a Lehmer generator modulo 65 537 on RandomSeed, which NewLandscape sets from the gene; every random choice of the landscape, the growth, the trips and the disasters comes from it (the tornado sprite alone uses LD A,R).
$5CC4 w RandomSeed 1 ; The random seed, 16 bits; NewLandscape sets it to the gene (Gene $6A8C); read directly by RandomDisaster, RadiationCell, Poll and NewCityItem (the new gene)

$5CC6 c Random ; The generator: seed' = (254 (seed + 1) - 1) mod 65 537, 254 a primitive root: period 65 536; A = the seed's low byte
> RandomSeed $5CC4 holds the seed. The arithmetic: H = the seed's low byte, L = $FD, so A:HL = 256 seed + 253; two SBC HL,DE take 2 seed off (254 seed + 253 in 24 bits), then the high byte is subtracted from the low 16 bits, which is mod 65537 because 65536 = -1; a borrow adds 1. Registers but A are kept. L1 343 327: the noise, the landscape, the simulation's random choices, the disasters.
$5CE1 RandomStore
$5CE4 ; LD A,H: dead, the next LD A,(RandomSeed) overwrites it

@module cursor ; The pointer and the map box
@about The arrow pointer drawn over the screen with its saved background, the box of the tool's size it shows over the view, and the tool palette's icons.
$5CEC b PointerShape 8 ; The pointer: an arrow, 8 pixel rows ORed over the screen (src/res/graphics/Pointer.png)
> Read by DrawPointer $5CFE (L1 156 303 reads of each byte).
@res graphics/Pointer.bin
$5CF4 PointerMask ; the mask ANDed with the screen before the arrow is ORed in
$5CFC b PointerX 1 ; the pointer's column in half characters, 0-62 (stage 1's "cursor")
$5CFD PointerY ; the pointer's row in pixels, 0-184 in steps of 2

$5CFE c DrawPointer ; Draw the pointer at its character cell: save the 8 bytes under it, AND the mask, OR the arrow
> The cell is row PointerY / 8, column PointerX / 2: the pointer moves on the character grid (no shift), though its coordinates are finer. The saved bytes go to PointerSave $5D34, the address to PointerAt $5D3C. Called by the UI loop every pass with ErasePointer after the pass's drawing (L1 158 080).
$5D1D DrawPointerLoop

$5D34 b PointerSave 8 ; The 8 screen bytes under the pointer, and their address
$5D3C PointerAt ; the display file address of the pointer's cell

$5D3E c ErasePointer ; Put the 8 saved bytes back at PointerAt
> L1 158 076.
$5D46 ErasePointerLoop

$5D4F b BoxSize 2 ; The map cursor's box: width and height in cells (the tool's size, ToolSizes)
> Written by ShowTool $6D90; read by TrackBox (L1 81 184 reads).
$5D51 BoxCell ; the pointer's cell in the view: column (0-31) and row (0-15)
$5D53 PointerInView ; nonzero when the pointer is on the map view (PointerY $40 or more)
$5D54 BoxShown ; 1 when the box is drawn (it fits on the view)

$5D55 c TrackBox ; Work out the pointer's view cell; when the tool's box fits on the view, invert it there
> Output: PointerInView, BoxCell, BoxShown; the box is the BoxSize cells from the pointer's cell, inverted by InvertBox (twice a pass: on, then off once drawn). L1 316 156, 40 592 on the view.

$5D8F c InvertBox ; Invert (XOR $FF) the BoxSize box at row B, column C of the screen
> The tool palette's highlight (ShowTool) and the map cursor (TrackBox). L1 41 241.
$5DA0 InvertBoxLine
$5DA4 InvertBoxByte

$5DB3 c DrawIcon2x2 ; Draw icon tiles A, A+1 (row B) and A+2, A+3 (row B+1) at columns C, C+1
> L1 9086.

$5DD2 c DrawToolIcons ; The tool palette: 14 icons of 2 x 2 tiles at row 1, columns 2-28, tiles 0, 4, 8 ...
> L1 649 (each tool change redraws it).
$5DD9 DrawToolIconsLoop

@module print ; Printing: characters, inline codes, numbers, frames, the status panel and the texts
@about PrintInline and its codes (FF row column, FB attribute, FD box, FC number, FE end), the decimal printers, DrawBox, the status panel (menu bar, city name, funds, date, tool), MonthNames and GameTexts with FindText and PrintPadded.
$5DEB c PrintChar ; Print character A at PrintAddr (JP to PrintGlyph $F754)
> L1 163 852.

$5DEF c PrintInline ; Print the codes that follow the CALL, up to $FE, then return after them
> The codes: $FF row column (position), $FB attribute (PrintAttr), $FD row column width height (a box, DrawBox), $FC number (printed as up to 3 digits; not used in any recording), $FE the end (JP to the byte after it); any other byte a character. PrintCol counts up. Used for every fixed text and window frame (L1 371 calls).
$5DF0 PrintInlineNext
$5E0F PrintInlineSkip
$5E1E PrintInlineAttr ; $FB n: PrintAttr = n
$5E25 PrintInlineNumber ; $FC n: n in 3 digits (L1 -)
$5E78 PrintInlineEnd ; $FE: return after the codes
$5E7A PrintInlineAt ; $FF row column
$5E88 PrintInlineBox ; $FD row column width height

$5E12 c NextColumn ; PrintAddr and PrintCol one column on
> L1 450 406.

$5E31 b PrintSpare 4 ; Four bytes no code reads (LD ($F751),BC: a cut instruction of the source); kept as bytes

$5E35 c PrintNumberAt ; HL in 5 digits at PrintCol, PrintRow
> L1 2 (the gene's 5 digits).
$5E41 c Print5Digits ; HL in 5 digits, leading zeros printed
> L1 4.
$5E47 c Print4Digits ; HL in 4 digits (the year)
> L1 9921.
$5E4D c Print3Digits ; HL in 3 digits (menus' numbers, the tax rate)
> L1 11 961.
$5E5E c PrintDigitOf ; One digit: count how often DE (a negative power of ten) goes into HL, then print it
$5E5F DigitCount
$5E66 c PrintDigit ; Print the digit A (0-9) at PrintAddr and step on
> L1 45 808.

$5E95 b BoxDims 2 ; DrawBox's box: width and height in characters

$5E97 c DrawBox ; Draw a window frame from icon tiles $38-$3F: row, column, width, height at (HL)
> The corners and edges are icon tiles: top $38 $39 $3A, sides $3F $3B, bottom $3E $3D $3C; the inside is left alone. Sets PrintCol/PrintRow at the corner. It also patches $5F0F (BoxRowRight+9) with the box's column, a fourth self-modified operand. L1 193.
$5EBB DrawBoxSides

$5ED2 b BoxSpare 1 ; One byte no code reads; kept as bytes

$5ED3 c DrawBoxRow ; One row of a frame: tile A, BoxDims-2 tiles B (a space: skipped), tile C, then PrintRow + 1
> The three tiles are written into the operands of the three LD A,n below (self-modified, $5EE9, $5EF9, $5F07). L1 3154.
$5EE8 BoxRowLeft ; LD A,left
$5EF3 BoxRowMiddle
$5EF8 BoxRowFill ; LD A,middle: $20 is left out (the box's inside)
$5F06 BoxRowRight ; LD A,right

$5F13 c DrawStatusPanel ; Draw the menu bar, the status box, the city name, funds, tool palette, tool name and date
> The menu bar "SYSTEM OPTIONS DISASTERS WINDOWS" at row 0 (attribute 7), the box rows 3-7, then DrawNameFunds, DrawToolIcons, ShowTool, PrintToolName, PrintDate, PrintFunds. L1 34 (the first draw and after each window).
$5F16 b StatusBarText 8 ; PrintInline codes: the menu bar (SYSTEM OPTIONS DISASTERS WINDOWS)
@res text/inline/StatusBar.bin
$5F40 c DrawStatusBox ; The status box: rows 3-7 of the top third (attribute $38), then the name, the tools, the date, the funds
$5F43 b StatusBoxText 10 ; PrintInline codes: the status box
$5F4D c DrawStatusItems ; The name, the funds, the tool palette, the tool name and the date
$5F60 c DrawNameFunds ; Print the city name (12 letters, CityName $5F68) at row 5, column 1, and "FUNDS" with the dollar sign at row 5, column 17
> CityName lies inside the inline text: typing a name writes these bytes (InputName $A6F9), so the text prints the current name. L1 35.
$5F63 b CityNameAt 5 ; PrintInline codes: attribute $39, row 5, column 1 (the name follows)
$5F68 CityName ; the city's name, 12 letters, "HERESVILLE  " in the image; InputName edits only 11 and the 12th letter is not saved (SaveCityName holds 11)
$5F7F c DrawNameFundsEnd ; RET

$5F80 c PrintFunds ; Print Funds ($8F97, 24 bits) at row 5, column 24
> L1 9918: every month and every purchase.

$5F8A t MonthNames ; The twelve months, three letters each, bit 7 on the last
@res text/MonthNames.bin

$5FAE c PrintDate ; Print the month (MonthNames by Month $8F9B) at row 4, column 23 and the year ($8F9C) after it
> L1 9917.

$5FC6 t GameTexts ; The tool names with prices, the levels, the six city classes, the 27 advice and disaster messages
> 50 strings, bit 7 on each last letter, found by FindText $6317 by index: 0-13 the tools ("BULLDOZE" with its price, 1 dollar, ... "AIRPORT", 10 000 dollars), 14-16 the levels (EASY, MEDIUM, HARD), 17-22 the classes (VILLAGE ... MEGALOPOLIS), 23 a space, 24-49 the advice and disasters ("MORE RESIDENTIAL ZONES NEEDED" ... "TORNADO DETECTED"). Read by PrintToolName, the level name, the class name and the advice (L1 1 602 984 reads, every byte).
@res text/GameTexts.bin

$6305 c PrintToolName ; Print the current tool's name and price (GameTexts by CurrentTool) at row 4, column 1, padded to 20 columns
> L1 99.

$6317 c FindText ; HL = the A-th (from 0) bit-7-ended string of the table at HL
> L1 14 996.
$631C FindTextLoop
$6327 FindTextNext

$632A c PrintPadded ; Print the string at HL at row B, column C, then spaces to A + 1 columns (C counts down from A for each letter but the last)
> L1 14 996.
$6335 PrintPaddedLoop
$634A PrintPaddedPad
$634F PrintPaddedSpace

@module clicks ; The pointer's movement and the clicks
@about MovePointer by the keys (and so the joysticks), Click on SPACE: the palette, the scroll corner, the map (MapClick) or the menu bar (OpenMenuAt).
$635C c MovePointer ; Move the pointer by the keys: O left, P right, Q up, A down (the joysticks come in as these)
> One step a pass for each key held. L1 158 076.

$637D c PointerRight ; PointerX + 1 up to 62
> L1 1895.
$638A c PointerLeft ; PointerX - 1 down to 0
> L1 2044.
$6397 c PointerUp ; PointerY - 2 down to 0
> The test of key 6 on the view (KeyRow0 bit 4) leads to a branch no recording ran (PointerUpStop, L1 -), which stops at row $40.
$63A6 PointerUpMove
$63AE PointerUpStop
$63B8 c PointerDown ; PointerY + 2 up to 184
$63C4 PointerDownStop

$63C9 b ClickCell 2 ; The pointer's character cell when SPACE was pressed: column (0-31), row (0-23)
$63CB ClickSpare ; two bytes no code reads

$63CD c Click ; SPACE pressed: take the pointer's cell, then the tool palette, the scroll arrows, the menu bar, the map
> With SwitchOff set (no task switch while the UI changes the screen and the map): ToolClick $6D51, ScrollClick $6CBF, MenuClick $6401, MapClick $7012. L1 609 (the presses of the recordings).

$63F9 c WaitSpaceUp ; Wait until SPACE is released
> L1 709 255 loop runs, 196 calls.

$6401 c MenuClick ; On row 0, open the menu under the pointer and wait for SPACE up
> L1 609, 69 on row 0.

$640E c OpenMenuAt ; The menu by the pointer's column: SYSTEM (below 14), OPTIONS (below 30), DISASTERS (below 48), WINDOWS
> IX = SystemMenu $A02B, OptionsMenu $A32F, DisastersMenu $A43E or WindowsMenu $A4C2, then ShowMenu $66F2. L1 69.

@module renderer ; The map view renderer
@about The view: 32 x 16 cells from ViewCol, ViewRow, one row per UI pass (DrawViewRowPass) or all at once (DrawView). A map byte below $80 is a cell tile (CellTiles $EA68, CellAttrs $F530); $80 and up starts a building row whose tiles come from BuildingTiles $ED58 and BuildingAttrs $F58E. Unpowered zone centres blink, roads show their traffic.
$6433 b Blink 1 ; Pass bit 4 at the start of the view row's draw: the blink phase

$6434 c DrawViewRowPass ; Draw one of the view's 16 rows, the one PassCount AND 15 picks
> So the view is redrawn every 16 passes of the UI loop, a row a pass, and the blinking cells change with PassCount bits 4 and 5. L1 158 073.

$6462 w TileIndex 1 ; The tile number being drawn

$6464 c DrawBlankCell ; Draw cell tile $3B (the "no power" lightning) instead: the on phase of the blink of an unpowered zone or building
$6468 c DrawCell ; Draw one view cell: map byte in A (or the building row in progress), tile and attribute
> A map byte below $80 is a cell tile at CellTiles $EA68 + 8 n with its attribute at CellAttrs $F530 + n; inside a building row (BuildingLeft not 0) the next building tile is drawn instead (DrawBuildingTile). Cells $54-$56 blink (BlinkCell); roads 7 and 8 show their traffic (RoadTraffic). The tile goes to ViewAt ($6482), the attribute through PutAttr. L1 5 379 061.
$6475 DrawCellTile
$6481 DrawTileAt ; LD DE,n: the screen address of the cell (operand $6482, ViewAt)
$6486 DrawTileLoop

$6499 c BlinkCell ; Cells $54-$56 (the unpowered zone centres) blink: with PassCount bit 5 set they show tile $3B (the lightning)
> L1 4 249 059, 11 661 blanked.

$64A9 c RoadTraffic ; Road cells 7 and 8: heavy traffic shows tile $34 / $36, medium blinks to $35 / $37
> Reads the traffic map at the cell (MapToRowCol $7635, TrafficAt $7581): $DC or more heavy, $96-$DB medium (the second tile when Blink is set). L1 668 762.
$64B5 RoadTrafficLook
$64D3 RoadTrafficTile

$64D5 b BuildingDummy 1 ; A byte IX points at while a building row is drawn (no reader of its value)

$64D6 c DrawBuildingTile ; The next tile of the building row in progress: BuildingTiles $ED58 + 8 n, attribute $F58E + n
> With PassCount bit 5, the first drawn cell of a building row whose start byte AND $F0 is $A0 (a top row, power bit 4 clear) shows tile $3B, the lightning. IX then points at BuildingDummy, so the row's other cells and the lower rows never blink: only the top-left cell of an unpowered building does. L1 1 130 002.
$64EF DrawBuildingAttr

$6505 w CellAttrPtr 1 ; The attribute table of the cell being drawn (CellAttrs or BuildingAttrs)
$6507 ViewCol ; the view's left column on the map, 0-64
